  "implications_for_r001": [
    "The old draft's denominators were wrong: it used the on-disk 24418 rows / 7620 statements as the deployment population, while the sampler saw the filtered 24246 rows / 7613 statements.",
    "Statements whose every row exceeds max_prompt_length have exposure probability EXACTLY 0 (7 of them). They can never appear in any historical label, so a model fit on V1 rollouts has never been asked about them - and they are only legitimate R001 test theorems if R001 can actually generate them, i.e. under the same 1024-token rule.",
    "Because V1 sampled rows, the labelled set over-represents high-multiplicity statements: mean pool multiplicity is 3.204 rows/statement, and the mean multiplicity of the 612 statements V1 actually rolled out is 11.315. A controller fit on those labels is fit under a multiplicity-weighted marginal, so applying it UNIFORMLY over a clean statement pool (owner \u00a77) is a covariate shift that R001 must state, not hide.",
    "Row-unit sampling with RandomSampler is WITHOUT replacement within an epoch; at 4 draws/step over the filtered population a repeat inside one step is essentially impossible, which is why V1's own batches never contained duplicate theorems.",
    "SAMPLER ACCOUNT REPRODUCES HISTORY: for every seed tested, V1's realized 240 prompt rows are exactly the first 240 of randperm(filtered_len, generator=manual_seed(data.get('seed', 1))) mapped through the filtered row order (3/3 set matches), AND every step holds the same 4 theorems (3/3 seeds, 180 steps). The only residual difference is the ORDER OF THEOREMS INSIDE a step, which is an artifact of build_records() sorting each step's groups rather than anything the trainer did (seeds where the realized sequence equals the within-step-sorted replay: 3/3). The row-unit RandomSampler is therefore an empirical, bit-exact account of V1 and not merely a reading of its source, so R001's uniform-over-clean-statements draw is a genuine change of measure rather than a guess about what V1 did."
  ],
